save(i,dict(title='Computing measures of all regular languages of infinite trees',formal='Historical question, resolved affirmatively: is there a terminating algorithm that, given any nondeterministic parity automaton A on labelled infinite binary trees, computes exactly the probability that a tree belongs to L(A), when every node label is chosen uniformly and independently from the finite input alphabet? The source asks to extend its weak-automaton result to all regular tree languages, equivalently all languages definable in full monadic second-order logic. The LICS 2023 theorem establishes this extension. It also covers the source’s generalization to finitely described rational stochastic branching processes.',definitions='A full binary tree is a map t:{L,R}*→Σ for a finite nonempty alphabet Σ; the empty word is the root. Specifying labels at k distinct nodes has probability |Σ|^{−k}; these cylinder probabilities determine the completed product measure. An automaton is a finite tuple (Σ,Q,q₀,Δ,Ω), with states Q, initial state q₀, transitions Δ⊆Q×Σ×Q×Q, and integer priorities Ω:Q→{0,…,d−1}. A run ρ assigns a state to every node, starts at q₀, and satisfies (ρ(v),t(v),ρ(vL),ρ(vR))∈Δ. It accepts if on every infinite branch the largest priority appearing infinitely often is even. A tree belongs to L(A) if there exists such an accepting run; the random choices generate the input tree, not a run. All finite tables are given explicitly, with integers in binary. Exact output may be a first-order formula over (R,0,1,+,×,≤) with one free real variable whose unique satisfying value is the required probability; real quantifiers and polynomial equalities and inequalities have their usual meanings. The result ensures this value is algebraic. For the branching-process extension, an input finite alphabet has an arity assigned to each label. A finite set of process states has an initial state, and each state carries a rational probability distribution on finitely many tuples (a,r₁,…,rₛ), where s is the arity of a. At a node in that state, an independent draw chooses its label and the states of its ordered children. Rational numerators and denominators are binary encoded. The automaton uses transitions (q,a,q₁,…,qₛ), with the same parity condition on every infinite branch and a valid transition at each leaf. The task is the probability that the resulting, possibly finite, tree admits an accepting run.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='A uniform terminating exact-measure algorithm for every finite parity tree automaton, including the rational branching-process input variant, answers the source question affirmatively. No weak or deterministic acceptance restriction is allowed. Exact output must identify the measure uniquely and permit comparison with rational thresholds; numerical estimates or the measurability theorem alone are insufficient. The source asks for computability, without a target optimal running time.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Random inputs, existential acceptance',text='The probability concerns which generated trees have some run satisfying the parity condition on every branch. It is not the acceptance probability of an automaton whose transitions themselves are randomly chosen.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The older theorem had a genuine restriction',text='The 2020 result treats weak alternating automata and weak monadic second-order logic, in which quantified node sets are finite. Full monadic second-order logic permits arbitrary node sets. The later parity-automaton theorem covers that larger class.',citation='full'),dict(title='The general question was subsequently settled',text='The LICS 2023 result computes an algebraic measure for every regular tree language. The authors’ expanded version explicitly states both the independent uniform-label case and the rational branching-process case in Theorem 1.2 and Corollary 1.3.',citation='full'),dict(title='Publication and representation',text='The full version appeared in the Journal of the ACM in 2026. Its exact algebraic answer need not be rational, so a finite real-arithmetic definition is an appropriate exact representation. This resolves the old computability question without claiming tight complexity bounds.',citation='journal')],why='Extends exact probabilistic analysis of branching behaviours from restricted specifications to the full expressive power of regular tree languages.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='Measures become computable for weak alternating automata; extension to general automata remains open.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2023',text='The Probabilistic Rabin Tree Theorem, published at LICS, resolves full regular-tree measure computability.',citation='solution'),dict(date='2024-11-21',text='Expanded version states and details the all-parity and rational branching-process results.',citation='full'),dict(date='2026-08-11',text='Publisher-deposited metadata records online publication of the full version in the Journal of the ACM.',citation='journal')],status='resolved',status_note='Matched the 2020 generalization question to the published LICS 2023 result and read the expanded version’s Theorem 1.2, Corollary 1.3, automaton and probability conventions, and Section 13. Verified the JACM DOI, authors and publication date through publisher-deposited Crossref metadata. No independent proof audit or proof-assistant certification is claimed.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A general computability theorem for exact probabilistic verification of regular branching specifications. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Replaced the truncated technique question with its recovered mathematical target, defined the probability space and full parity acceptance, and marked resolved by the direct later theorem.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['A tree language describes which labelled branching structures satisfy a specification.','The source asks whether its probability can be computed for every regular language, beyond weak automata.','The probability is over independent uniform labels, or more generally a finite rational branching process.','A 2023 theorem computes an exact algebraic answer for arbitrary parity tree automata.','The full version, published in JACM in 2026, resolves the original computability question.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','solution','full','journal'])),notes=['Recovered general regular-tree computability target and removed technique framing.','Defined max-parity runs, completed product measure, rational branching process and exact output.','Matched published 2023 solution and 2024 full theorem; verified 2026 journal metadata; marked resolved.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://lics.siglog.org/lics23/accepted.php','https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1145/3819061'])
